    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full `mvn test` run reports **BUILD SUCCESS** with all tests passing.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `namesakeCount` following the repo's existing `customerCode`/`householdId` pattern \u2014 a persisted owner field computed by a small pipeline function at create time and auto-mapped into the response DTO.\n\nChanges:\n1. **`CountNamesakes.java`** (new) \u2014 one cohesive function class (WMC_other = 0). Iterates existing owners before save and counts those sharing the same first + last name case-insensitively, then records it on the owner. Because it runs before `SaveOwner`, the new owner is excluded from its own count.\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired the `countNamesakes` step into the create pipeline (after `assignCode`, before `save`), under the same `transaction` governance.\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` field with a `namesake_count` column plus a plain getter/setter (each CC 1). MapStruct then maps it into `OwnerDto` automatically by name \u2014 no mapper change needed.\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `namesakeCount` (integer, int32) property to the `Owner` schema so the generated DTO exposes it.\n5. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** + **`db/hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column (matching the precedent that these evolving columns live only in the test DB schema) and a trailing `NULL` value to the 10 positional seed inserts so they still match the column count.\n\nStructural impact was kept low by putting the new behaviour in its own tiny class rather than growing an existing large one, and by relying on MapStruct's by-name mapping to avoid touching the mapper.",
